the usual arithmetic conversions, and the literal types they act on.

promote_type answers "which type wins" -- wider first, unsigned at equal width
-- and its own note says that is "not the full usual arithmetic conversions, it
is the selection rule those conversions need under them". This is the part that
was under them.

Three things were missing, and only the first is what case 110 probes.

1. A CONSTANT'S TYPE. C89 3.1.3.2 gives a constant the first type it fits, and
   the BASE and SUFFIX choose which list it fits into:

       decimal, no suffix     int, long, unsigned long
       hex/octal, no suffix   int, unsigned int, long, unsigned long
       u/U                    unsigned int, unsigned long
       l/L                    long, unsigned long
       u and l                unsigned long

   unsigned int is on two of those lists and absent from two, which is the
   whole subtlety: 0x87654321 is unsigned int, 2271560481 is long, and
   0x87654321L is long. Everything was typed `integer` regardless.

2. THE CONVERSION ITSELF. Having chosen the type, both operands have to BE it
   before the operator runs. An int against an unsigned int must keep its low
   32 bits; micro-c left it sign-extended in a 64-bit register.

3. THE RESULT. unsigned int arithmetic is modulo 2^32, and a 64-bit register
   keeps bits the type does not have.

   AND FOUR BYTES ONLY, WHICH THIS PATCH GOT WRONG ONCE AND SHIPPED. The
   integer promotions run first, so there is no `unsigned char` arithmetic in
   C -- both operands become int and so does the result. promote_type picks
   BETWEEN the operands and cannot promote past both, so for two narrow
   operands it returns the narrow type, and narrowing the result to it threw
   away every bit above the eighth:

       unsigned char a = 200, b = 100;   a + b   C says 300, micro-c said 44

   mc-tcc then SEGFAULTED assembling musl's aarch64 memset.S and memcpy.S --
   tcc's ARM64 assembler builds instruction words out of narrow unsigned
   fields. libc.a came out with no memset and no memcpy, every hosted link
   failed on `undefined symbol`, and the stage-4 ladder stopped at rung 3
   having previously reached rung 6.
   emit_usual_arithmetic_conversion always had the four-byte guard;
   emit_result_in_chosen_type was written from it and the line did not carry
   over.

WHAT FOUND THEM, AND WHY THE CASE COULD NOT. Case 110 has four probes and was
written FROM this bug, so it passing proves little -- MICRO-C.md's own note is
that "a test suite written from bugs already found measures what has been
fixed, not what remains". tools/uac-sweep.sh generates the space instead:
every combination of nine operand types, fifteen literal spellings, twelve
operators and eight values, with gcc as the oracle. 12,960 points per
architecture, about ninety seconds.

AND THE SWEEP DID NOT CATCH THE ONE THAT SHIPPED, which is the sharper lesson.
Every point paired a variable with a LITERAL, and a literal is never narrower
than int -- so promote_type always had a four-byte operand and the narrow-pair
case was outside the space. It also compared `(x op lit) ? 1 : 0`, a
PREDICATE, so 300 and 44 both read as true. Both holes are closed: the sweep
now folds the actual value to five bits and has a second phase over pairs of
VARIABLES.

It found four things after case 110 was already green, in this order:

    0x87654321L        typed unsigned int, so the other operand zero-extended
                       where C sign-extends -- the OPPOSITE answer, and the
                       same defect 110 is about, one suffix over
    0x87654321UL       the same for the unsigned-long list
    char vs UINT_MAX   the integer promotions run FIRST, so an operand
                       narrower than int converts with everything else; the
                       first version required size 4 exactly and missed all of
                       char, signed char, unsigned char, short, unsigned short
    char * 0x80000000  the product kept bits unsigned int does not have
    char + 0xFFFFFFFF  + and - have their own path for pointer scaling and
                       never reached the conversion at all

SCOPE, WHICH WAS WRONG ONCE AND LOUDLY. The conversion first went into
common_recursion. Ten sites call that, and in most of them REGISTER_ONE does
not hold a VALUE: assignment leaves the destination ADDRESS there, and so do
array indexing and the pointer-scaling paths. Truncating a stack address to 32
bits made `local.n = 5` on a struct segfault. It belongs to the operators
whose contract is "two values in, one value out" -- general_recursion,
arithmetic_recursion, and the two additive branches by name.

The guards are what keep it off everything else: both operands must be four
bytes or narrower, so pointers are excluded by being register-width and
structs by having members; the chosen type must be a four-byte unsigned
integer, so the pointer results the additive branches set deliberately pass
straight through emit_result_in_chosen_type untouched.

VOCABULARY. REGISTER_ONE is the left operand and neither table could narrow
it. `mov_ebx,ebx` and `mov_w1,w1` are added by the m2libc series alongside
this. Which side is signed is not fixed -- `got != LIT` and `LIT != got` are
both legal C -- so both registers are converted rather than one guessed at.

SILENT ON THE OTHER ARCHITECTURES, deliberately. micro-c targets amd64 and
aarch64 here; x86, armv7l and riscv keep exactly the behaviour they had rather
than being handed an encoding nobody has assembled.

